    45	\begin{document}
    46	
    47	\begin{abstract}
    48	We give a deterministic algorithm that receives only an $n$-bit integer $N$, uses
    49	$N^{1/6+5\varepsilon/6}(\log N)^{O(1)}$ bit operations on every input, never outputs anything
    50	other than a proper divisor of $N$ or \texttt{FAIL}, and factors all but an
    51	$O(N^{-\varepsilon})$ fraction of the semiprimes $N=pq$ with primes $p<q\le 2p$
    52	(for any fixed $\varepsilon\in(0,1/60]$ and $n\ge n_0(\varepsilon)$). Taking polylogarithmic
    53	weight heights instead gives cost $N^{1/6}(\log N)^{O(1)}$ with an exceptional fraction
    54	$O((\log N)^{-A})$ for any fixed $A$. The best proved deterministic exponent for arbitrary
    55	inputs remains $1/5$ (Harvey; Harvey--Hittmeir). The algorithm is a Lehman-type weighted
    56	collision search. It takes the McKee--Pinch divisibility event $\ell\mid Bp-Aq$ over all
    57	primes $\ell$ in four dyadic bands near $N^{1/6}$ and all small coprime weights $(A,B)$,
    58	together with the Harvey--Hittmeir modulus-squared lock. One baby-step list is shared by
    59	every band prime through cofactor-powered handles. Three ingredients carry the proof. The
    60	collision modulo $p$ is an exact identity, so no lower bound on any multiplicative order is
    61	needed. Four disjoint bands guarantee one band coprime to $q-1$, and on that band a
    62	two-sided (useless) collision forces a rare congruence modulo $\ord_q(2)$. The arithmetic
    63	large sieve bounds the uncovered pairs. We also record a matching lower bound of order
    64	$N^{1/6}$ for covering families in a one-base sumset collision-search model, and
    65	pre-registered experiments. At 40--80 bits the measured cost grows more slowly than that
    66	of a calibrated Harvey--Hittmeir implementation (slope $0.203$ against $0.226$ in $\log_2$
    67	cost per bit), but Harvey--Hittmeir is still $2.3$--$4.9$ times cheaper at those sizes. The
    68	result says nothing about the worst case. Every ingredient is prior art; we found no prior
    69	formulation of this combination.
    70	\end{abstract}
