    85	\section{Introduction and statement of results}
    86	%======================================================================
    87	
    88	\subsection{Background}
    89	Deterministic integer factoring with rigorous bounds has a short list of exponents. Trial
    90	division costs $N^{1/2}$. Lehman's method \cite{lehman1974} and the Pollard--Strassen
    91	method \cite{pollard1974,strassen1976} give $N^{1/3+o(1)}$ and $N^{1/4+o(1)}$, and the
    92	latter was refined by Bostan--Gaudry--Schost \cite{bgs2007} and Costa--Harvey
    93	\cite{costaharvey}. Hittmeir \cite{hittmeir2018} saved a subexponential factor at exponent
    94	$1/4$, and then proved exponent $2/9$ \cite{hittmeir2021} with a time--space trade-off for
    95	Lehman's method. Harvey \cite{harvey2021} proved exponent $1/5$, and Harvey--Hittmeir
    96	\cite{hh2022} improved the logarithmic factor; Gao, Feng, Hu and Pan \cite{gao2025} improve
    97	logarithmic factors again for balanced semiprimes, at the same exponent $1/5$. Below $1/5$,
    98	the known routes are conditional: Umans and Wang \cite{umanswang2025} showed that a
    99	number-theoretic divisor-cover conjecture would give exponent $1/6$, and He and Sahai
   100	\cite{hesahai2026} refuted that conjecture's one-dimensional arithmetic-progression form.
   101	
   102	This paper is about the \emph{average} case over the hardest natural population, semiprimes
   103	with two factors of comparable size. For unrestricted semiprimes the average case is easy:
   104	almost all of them have a factor below $N^{\delta}$, so trial division handles them (see
   105	\cite{crisanerban} for the counting). Balanced semiprimes are the opposite. Every method
   106	above is analysed in the worst case, and we know of no proved average-case exponent below
   107	$1/5$ for them.
   108	
   109	\subsection{Population and main result}
   110	Let $n$ be the bit length, $X=2^n$, $P=2^{n/2}$, and
   111	\[
   112	  \Sn=\{(p,q): p<q\le 2p,\ p,q\ \text{prime},\ 2^{n-1}\le pq<2^n\}.
   113	\]
   114	We count $\Sn$ by pairs. By unique factorisation this is the same as counting the
   115	semiprimes $N=pq$. For $(p,q)\in\Sn$ we have $p\in[\sqrt{N/2},\sqrt N]\subset[P/2,P]$ and
   116	$q\in(P/\sqrt2,\sqrt2P)$. The prime number theorem gives $|\Sn|\ge c_0P^2/n^2$ for an
   117	absolute $c_0>0$ and all large $n$.
   118	
   119	\begin{theorem}[Theorem D$'$; proved]\label{thm:main}
   120	Fix $\varepsilon\in(0,1/60]$. There are $n_0(\varepsilon)$ and absolute constants $C,c$
   121	such that for every $n\ge n_0$ the deterministic algorithm $\DCL_{n,\varepsilon}$ of
   122	Section~\ref{sec:alg} has the following properties.
   123	\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
   124	\item \emph{Correctness.} On every input $N$ it outputs either a proper divisor $d$ of $N$
   125	  ($1<d<N$, $d\mid N$) or \texttt{FAIL}.
   126	\item \emph{Cost.} On every $n$-bit input it uses $O\bigl(n^{c}\,2^{(1/6+5\varepsilon/6)n}\bigr)
   127	  = N^{1/6+5\varepsilon/6}(\log N)^{O(1)}$ bit operations.
   128	\item \emph{Success.} It outputs \texttt{FAIL} on at most $C\,2^{-\varepsilon n}|\Sn|$ pairs
   129	  $(p,q)\in\Sn$.
   130	\end{enumerate}
   131	Equivalently, for every $\delta\in(0,1/72]$ there is a deterministic algorithm of cost
   132	$N^{1/6+\delta+o(1)}$ whose exceptional fraction on $\Sn$ is $O(N^{-6\delta/5})$.
   133	\end{theorem}
   134	
   135	\begin{corollary}[Corollary D$''$; proved]\label{cor:polylog}
   136	For every fixed $a>0$, the same algorithm with weight height $H=\lceil n^{1+a}\rceil$ costs
   137	$N^{1/6}(\log N)^{O(1+a)}$ bit operations and fails on $O((\log N)^{-2a})$ of $\Sn$.
   138	\end{corollary}
   139	
   140	\begin{remark}[Scope]\label{rem:scope}
   141	We state the scope plainly, because each item is easy to over-read.
   142	\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
   143	\item The statement is about the true factor of an input drawn uniformly from $\Sn$, with
   144	  exceptions counted by pairs. It says nothing about any particular $N$. In particular it
   145	  is \emph{not} a worst-case bound, and it does not show that, for almost every $N$, every
   146	  integer candidate $p$ is covered (the ``full-candidate'' covering model of
   147	  Section~\ref{sec:lower}).
   148	\item Only ratios $q/p\le 2$ are covered. Ratios $q/p>2$ (and unbalanced semiprimes in
   149	  general) are not.
   150	\item ``Never wrong'' means: every output is validated as a proper divisor. The algorithm may
   151	  output \texttt{FAIL} on inputs outside $\Sn$ and on the exceptional pairs.
   152	\item The theorem is asymptotic ($n\ge n_0(\varepsilon)$). At practical sizes the algorithm
   153	  is slower than Harvey--Hittmeir (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}).
   154	\end{enumerate}
   155	\end{remark}
   156	
   157	\subsection{How it works, in one paragraph}
   158	Fix a prime $\ell$ and coprime weights $(A,B)$. If $\ell\mid Bp-Aq$, then $p\bmod\ell$ is
   159	one of the two square roots of $AN/B$ modulo $\ell$, and these roots are computable from
   160	$N$. On that residue class the weighted sum $Bp+Aq$ is determined modulo $\ell^2$ (the
   161	Harvey--Hittmeir lock), so $g=(Bp+Aq-c)/\ell^2$ is an integer in a window of length about
   162	$H\sqrt N/\ell^2$. Exponentiating, $2^{(L/\ell^2)(B+AN-c)}$ equals $\gamma^{g}$ modulo $p$,
   163	with $\gamma=2^{L}$ and $L=\prod\ell^2$ over the band. Every target, for every band prime,
   164	is therefore one giant step against a single baby list $\gamma^0,\dots,\gamma^{m-1}$, and a
   165	product-tree/remainder-tree evaluation detects collisions modulo the unknown $p$. Primes
   166	near $N^{1/6}$ balance the baby list ($\approx H\sqrt N/\ell^2$) against the number of
   167	targets ($\approx H^2\ell$). Coverage is a sieve problem in $p$ for fixed $q$, and the large
   168	sieve shows that almost every $p$ is caught. The collision modulo $p$ is exact, so no order
   169	lemma is needed there. It only remains to show that the true giant does not \emph{also}
   170	collide modulo $q$. Using four disjoint bands, one of them is coprime to $q-1$. On that band
   171	a two-sided collision forces $\ord_q(2)$ to divide a specific small expression, which a CRT
   172	count shows to be rare.
   173	
   174	\subsection{A lower bound in the collision-search model}
   175	The exponent $1/6$ is not an accident of parameter choice. In a model that covers Lehman,
   176	Hittmeir, Harvey, Harvey--Hittmeir and the present algorithm (one base, polynomial exponent
   177	maps of bounded degree, residue-class locks modulo any number of moduli, sumset
   178	baby-step/giant-step collision detection), every family that covers all integer candidates
   179	in the balanced range costs at least $cN^{1/6}$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:A}). With $N^{o(1)}$
   180	distinct moduli, or with targets of degree at least $2$, the bound is $N^{1/5-o(1)}$
   181	(Theorems~\ref{thm:Aprime} and~\ref{thm:B}). This is a lower bound \emph{in a model}, not
   182	for factoring. It explains why many moduli are necessary below $1/5$ and why $1/6$ is the
   183	floor of this approach.
   184	
   185	\subsection{What is new}
   186	Each ingredient is known (Section~\ref{sec:prior}). The divisibility event
   187	$\lambda p\equiv\pm\mu q\pmod k$ with small multipliers, and a heuristic balanced method
   188	over primes $k\approx N^{1/4}$, are in McKee and Pinch \cite[\S5]{mckeepinch1996}. The
   189	modulus-squared lock is in McKee \cite{mckee1999} and Harvey--Hittmeir
   190	\cite[Prop.~3.3]{hh2022}. Shared collision search with multipoint evaluation is in Harvey
   191	\cite[Alg.~4.1--4.2]{harvey2021}. The large sieve is Montgomery's
   192	\cite{montgomery1968,mv1973}. What this paper adds is the following combination:
   193	\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
   194	\item a band of primes at $N^{1/6}H^{-1/3}$ instead of $N^{1/4}$,
   195	\item made possible by one baby list shared across all band primes via cofactor-powered
   196	  handles, so that each target costs one giant step;
   197	\item a proof (rather than a probability heuristic) that the event occurs for all but
   198	  $O(N^{-\varepsilon})$ of the pairs: the prime-pair large sieve with growing weight height;
   199	\item one-sided extraction with no order hypothesis: the clean-band argument, the
   200	  elementary bound on primes with small $\ord_q(2)$, and a CRT count of two-sided true
   201	  collisions;
   202	\item a full bit-complexity accounting, and the matching model lower bound.
   203	\end{enumerate}
   204	We found no prior formulation of this combination.
   205	
   206	\subsection{Organisation}
   207	Section~\ref{sec:prior} reviews prior art. Section~\ref{sec:alg} defines the algorithm.
   208	Section~\ref{sec:proofs} proves Theorem~\ref{thm:main} and Corollary~\ref{cor:polylog}.
   209	Section~\ref{sec:lower} gives the lower bound in the collision-search model.
   210	Section~\ref{sec:experiments} reports pre-registered experiments. Section~\ref{sec:open}
   211	lists limitations and open problems.
   212	
   213	%======================================================================
   214	\section{Prior art}\label{sec:prior}
   215	%======================================================================
   216	
   217	We separate what we read ourselves from what we know only through an AI literature sweep.
   218	Items marked \emph{[sweep]} were checked against the source by the GPT-6.1-sol literature
   219	sweeps described in the Acknowledgements, not re-read by us. Items marked UNVERIFIED have
   220	bibliographic details we have not checked against the source.
   221	
   222	\paragraph{Lehman and weighted Fermat searches.}
   223	Lehman \cite{lehman1974} searches for $4kN=x^2-y^2$ over small multipliers $k$ and proves a
   224	deterministic $N^{1/3+o(1)}$ bound. Weighted Fermat searches go back to Lawrence
   225	\cite{lawrence1895}. The weighted sums $Bp+Aq$ of the present paper are Lehman's
   226	mechanism; we claim nothing new about them.
   227	
   228	\paragraph{McKee 1999.}
   229	McKee \cite{mckee1999} speeds up Fermat's method using a prime $m$ dividing the square-root
   230	variable, which restricts it modulo $m^2$; the resulting $N^{1/4+\eta}$ method is labelled
   231	heuristic by the author. Its \S9 points back to McKee--Pinch \S5 [sweep].
   232	
   233	\paragraph{McKee--Pinch 1996, \S5: a direct precursor.}
   234	We read \S5 (pp.~221--223) of \cite{mckeepinch1996} from the public scan indexed at
   235	\url{https://cr.yp.to/bib/1996/mckee-deterministic.html}. It contains the following.
   236	\begin{itemize}
   237	\item \emph{Lehmer's speed-up:} if $p\equiv q\equiv1\pmod k$, Fermat's method runs in
   238	  $O(q/k^2)$.
   239	\item \emph{Their extension:} for $p\equiv\pm q\pmod k$, Fermat runs in $O(q/k^{2-\epsilon})$,
   240	  writing $n=u^2-v^2$ with $u$ or $v$ determined modulo $k^2$ from square roots modulo $k$.
   241	  This is a modulus-squared lock.
   242	\item \emph{Multipliers:} if $\lambda p\equiv\pm\mu q\pmod k$, the same method splits
   243	  $\lambda\mu n$ in $O(\max(\lambda p,\mu q)/k^2\cdot n^{\epsilon})$.
   244	\item \emph{A deterministic $O(n^{1/3+\epsilon})$ algorithm}, with $k=2\lfloor n^{1/3}\rfloor+1$
   245	  and a pigeonhole lemma guaranteeing $\lambda\mu\le k$ with $\lambda p\equiv\pm\mu q\pmod k$.
   246	\item \emph{A heuristic balanced method}, described by the authors as ``not however an
   247	  algorithm'': take primes $k\in(n^{1/4},2n^{1/4})$ and small $\lambda,\mu$, and hope that
   248	  $\lambda p-\mu q\equiv0\pmod k$ for some of them. Each $(\lambda\mu,k)$ costs $O(n^{\epsilon})$,
   249	  and the expected time $O(n^{1/4+\epsilon})$ rests on the heuristic that $q-p$ has a prime
   250	  factor in the band with probability $\gg1/\log n$.
   251	\end{itemize}
   252	So the core event of the present paper (a band prime divides a small-weight combination
   253	$Bp-Aq$, with $N$-determined residues and a modulus-squared reduction) is theirs. Their
   254	per-$k$ cost is $O(n^\epsilon)$ because $q/k^2=O(1)$. We instead trade a larger window for
   255	fewer, smaller primes through a shared baby list, and we prove the coverage statement. The
   256	contributions (i)--(v) of the introduction are what we add.
   257	
   258	\paragraph{Hittmeir 2018 and 2021.}
   259	Hittmeir \cite{hittmeir2018} combines baby-step/giant-step with sieving of $p+q$ modulo many
   260	small primes and proves $N^{1/4}\exp(-\Omega(\log N/\log\log N))$ up to polylogarithmic
   261	factors. Hittmeir \cite{hittmeir2021} adapts Strassen-type polynomial collision detection to
   262	Lehman's weighted sums and proves $N^{2/9+o(1)}$. Modular-hyperbola sieving over many
   263	small primes is therefore old; what differs here is that each prime is used
   264	\emph{selectively} (only the two classes reached by a short weight vector) rather than to
   265	cover all classes.
   266	
   267	\paragraph{Harvey 2021; Harvey--Hittmeir 2022.}
   268	Harvey \cite{harvey2021} proves deterministic complexity $O(N^{1/5}\log^{16/5}N)$ with a
   269	single shared baby-step/giant-step search over Lehman's multiplier family, using
   270	congruences $\alpha^{aq+bp}\equiv\alpha^{aN+b}\pmod p$ and multipoint evaluation over
   271	$\Z/N\Z$ (Alg.~4.1--4.2). Harvey--Hittmeir \cite{hh2022} save a factor
   272	$(\log\log N)^{3/5}$; their Proposition~3.3 contains the congruence $aq-bp\equiv0\pmod m$
   273	and the modulus-squared lock that we use as Lemma~\ref{lem:lock}, with a single modulus
   274	and lattice-chosen weights. Harvey also remarks that exponent $1/6$ would need a ``fully
   275	square-root speedup'' of Lehman's search with $r\asymp N^{1/3}$ [sweep].
   276	
   277	\paragraph{Gao--Feng--Hu--Pan 2025.}
   278	For balanced semiprimes, \cite{gao2025} claims deterministic
   279	$O(N^{1/5}\log^{13/5}N/(\log\log N)^{3/5})$ using rank-3 lattices [sweep; statements checked,
   280	proofs not reproduced]. The exponent remains $1/5$.
   281	
   282	\paragraph{Umans--Wang 2025; He--Sahai 2026.}
   283	Umans and Wang \cite{umanswang2025} show that a Strong Prefactored Divisor Conjecture would
   284	give deterministic exponent $1/6$ (their Thms.~5.2 and~5.5). He and Sahai
   285	\cite{hesahai2026} refute the one-dimensional arithmetic-progression version at the proposed
   286	parameters $(1/3,1/3)$; higher-rank versions remain open [sweep]. Our result is
   287	unconditional but average-case. The covering statement we would need for a worst-case
   288	version (Conjecture~\ref{conj:DCH} below) is a different object: we know of no implication
   289	in either direction, and no transfer of the He--Sahai argument to it, but we have not
   290	proved that none exists.
   291	
   292	\paragraph{Oznovich--Volk 2025; Harvey--Hittmeir 2026.}
   293	These give deterministic algorithms that find an element of large multiplicative order
   294	modulo $N$ or a factor: Oznovich--Volk \cite{oznovichvolk2025} for target order
   295	$D\ge N^{1/6}$ in time $D^{1/2+o(1)}$, and Harvey--Hittmeir \cite{hh2026} for every $D$
   296	[sweep]. We do not use them. They produce an arbitrary element of large order, not one in
   297	the prescribed image $2^{L}$, and large order modulo $N$ does not imply large order modulo
   298	a specific prime $q$. Our clean-band argument avoids the issue.
   299	
   300	\paragraph{Carella, arXiv:1308.2891.}
   301	Carella \cite{carella} explicitly claims a deterministic factoring algorithm with
   302	$O(N^{1/6+\varepsilon})$ arithmetic operations for balanced $N=pq$. We have not re-derived
   303	its argument. \emph{The following assessment is that of the GPT-6.1-sol referee of our
   304	round 13, not ours:} the displayed proof loses a necessary primitive-polynomial
   305	normalisation; every coefficient of the correctly expanded polynomial is divisible by $B$,
   306	removing that content lowers the scaled height from about $N$ to $N^{5/6}$, and the cited
   307	small-root condition then no longer supports $X=Y=N^{1/3}$. On that assessment the referee
   308	did not regard the proof as establishing the bound. We list \cite{carella} as a claimed but
   309	(per that assessment) unproved prior statement of the same exponent, with a different
   310	(lattice) mechanism and a worst-case rather than average-case claim.
   311	
   312	\paragraph{The large sieve.}
   313	We use the arithmetic large sieve in Montgomery's form \cite{montgomery1968} with the
   314	Montgomery--Vaughan constant \cite{mv1973}; in fact only its prime terms are needed, and we
   315	derive them from the additive inequality in Lemma~\ref{lem:sieve}.
   316	
   317	\paragraph{Summary of the literature search.}
   318	Two independent GPT-6.1-sol sweeps and our own searches found no established,
   319	unconditional, oracle-free deterministic result below exponent $1/5$ that factors almost
   320	all bounded-ratio prime pairs. Almost-all results that rely on totient or elliptic-curve
   321	oracles exist but do not take only $N$ as input [sweep]. This is the outcome of a search,
   322	not a proof of priority.
   323	
   324	%======================================================================
   325	\section{The algorithm}\label{sec:alg}
   326	%======================================================================
   327	
   328	\subsection{Parameters}
   329	All parameters depend on $n$ and $\varepsilon$ only, never on $N$. Constant-factor
   330	conventions (floors and ceilings) do not affect the proofs.
   331	\begin{itemize}
   332	\item Weight height $H=\lceil n\,2^{\varepsilon n/2}\rceil$, and weight set
   333	  \[V=\{(A,B)\in[1,H]^2:\gcd(A,B)=1,\ A\ne B\}.\]
   334	  For large $H$, $H^2/2\le|V|\le H^2$.
   335	\item Band scale $Y=\lceil 2^{n/6}H^{-1/3}\rceil$.
   336	\item Four bands, $k=0,1,2,3$:
   337	  \[\Pk=\{\ell\ \text{prime}:\ \ell\equiv3\pmod 4,\ 2^kY<\ell\le2^{k+1}Y\},\qquad
   338	  L_k=\prod_{\ell\in\Pk}\ell^2.\]
   339	\item Window bound $\spanbar=\lfloor\tfrac52H(2^{n/2}+1)\rfloor+2H+3$ and
   340	  $\mbar=\lfloor\spanbar/(2^kY)^2\rfloor+2$.
   341	\item Base $\beta=2$.
   342	\end{itemize}
   343	We record the scale separations used below. As $n\to\infty$ with $\varepsilon\le1/60$
   344	fixed:
   345	\begin{equation}\label{eq:scales}
   346	  Y>H^2,\qquad 16Y<P/2,\qquad Y^4>\sqrt2\,P.
   347	\end{equation}
   348	The first needs $2^{n/6}>n^{7/3}2^{7\varepsilon n/6}$ (true for $\varepsilon<1/7$); the
   349	third needs $2^{n/6}>\sqrt2\,H^{4/3}$ (true for $\varepsilon<1/4$).
   350	
   351	\subsection{Steps}
   352	Input: $N$ with $n$ bits.
   353	\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
   354	\item If $N<4$ output \texttt{FAIL}. If $N$ is even output $2$. Put $a_0=\lceil\sqrt{N/2}\rceil$
   355	  and $b_0=\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor$; every $(p,q)\in\Sn$ has $p\in[a_0,b_0]$.
   356	\item For $v=(A,B)\in V$ let $F_v(x)=Bx+AN/x$, $\mathrm{Lo}_v=\lfloor2\sqrt{ABN}\rfloor$ and
   357	  $\mathrm{Hi}_v=\max_{x\in\{a_0,b_0\}}\lceil F_v(x)\rceil$. By AM--GM, $\mathrm{Lo}_v\le F_v(x)$
   358	  for all $x>0$, and by convexity $F_v\le\mathrm{Hi}_v$ on $[a_0,b_0]$. Also
   359	  $\mathrm{Hi}_v-\mathrm{Lo}_v\le(B+\sqrt2A)\sqrt N+2H+2\le\spanbar$.
   360	\end{enumerate}
   361	Then, for each block $k=0,1,2,3$:
   362	\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.,resume]
   363	\item \emph{Exponent tree.} Build the balanced integer product tree of $\{\ell^2:\ell\in\Pk\}$.
   364	  Push $2$ down the tree: a node holding $2^{L_k/\Pi(\text{node})}$ passes
   365	  $2^{L_k/\Pi(\text{node})\cdot\Pi(\text{sibling})}$ to each child. This yields
   366	  $u_\ell=2^{L_k/\ell^2}\bmod N$ for all $\ell\in\Pk$. Put $\gamma=u_{\ell_0}^{\ell_0^2}=2^{L_k}\bmod N$
   367	  for any $\ell_0\in\Pk$. If some $\ell\in\Pk$ divides $N$ and $1<\ell<N$, output $\ell$.
   368	\item \emph{Targets.} For $\ell\in\Pk$ and $v\in V$: let $a=ANB^{-1}\bmod\ell$. If
   369	  $a^{(\ell-1)/2}\not\equiv1\pmod\ell$, skip. Otherwise $r_0=a^{(\ell+1)/4}\bmod\ell$, and the
   370	  roots of $Br^2\equiv AN\pmod\ell$ are $r_0$ and $\ell-r_0$. For each root $r$ put
   371	  \[
   372	    s=Nr^{-1}\bmod\ell^2,\quad c=(Br+As)\bmod\ell^2,\quad
   373	    g_{\mathrm{lo}}=\Bigl\lceil\frac{\mathrm{Lo}_v-c}{\ell^2}\Bigr\rceil,\quad
   374	    g_{\mathrm{hi}}=\Bigl\lfloor\frac{\mathrm{Hi}_v-c}{\ell^2}\Bigr\rfloor,
   375	  \]
   376	  and $W_\tau=g_{\mathrm{hi}}-g_{\mathrm{lo}}+1\le\spanbar/\ell^2+1\le\mbar$. The triple
   377	  $\tau=(v,\ell,r)$ is a \emph{target}. Let $T_k\le2|V||\Pk|$ be the number of targets.
   378	\item \emph{Baby length.} Choose $m$ either as $m=\mbar$, or by the
   379	  \emph{endpoint-competitive} rule: minimise $m+\sum_\tau\lceil W_\tau/m\rceil$ over an integer
   380	  geometric grid whose last point is $W_{\max}=\max_\tau W_\tau\le\mbar$. Under either rule
   381	  $m\le\mbar$ and $m+T'\le\mbar+T_k$, where $T'=\sum_\tau\lceil W_\tau/m\rceil$.
   382	\item \emph{Giants.} Precompute $w_\ell=u_\ell^N$. For each target and $0\le j<\lceil W_\tau/m\rceil$:
   383	  \[
   384	    G_{\tau,j}=w_\ell^{A}\,u_\ell^{\,B-c+\ell^2}\,\gamma^{-(g_{\mathrm{lo}}+1)}\,\gamma^{-jm}
   385	    \equiv u_\ell^{\,B+AN-c}\,\gamma^{-(g_{\mathrm{lo}}+jm)}\pmod N,
   386	  \]
   387	  using $u_\ell^{\ell^2}=\gamma$; $\gamma$ is invertible because $N$ is odd.
   388	\item \emph{Collision detection.} Build $f(x)=\prod_{0\le i<m}(x-\gamma^i)\in(\Z/N\Z)[x]$ by a
   389	  product tree. Evaluate $f$ at all giants by a remainder tree; only monic divisors occur,
   390	  so this is valid over $\Z/N\Z$. For each giant compute $d=\gcd(f(G),N)$; if $1<d<N$ output
   391	  $d$.
   392	\end{enumerate}
   393	If no block outputs a divisor, output \texttt{FAIL}.
   394	
   395	\begin{remark}\label{rem:alg}
   396	(a) No multiplicative order is computed, assumed or certified, and no randomness is used.
   397	(b) The symmetric weight $A=B$ is excluded: by Lemma~\ref{lem:modq} its true giant always
   398	collides modulo $q$ too, so it can never give a one-sided collision.
   399	(c) The four bands are used only in Lemma~\ref{lem:clean}.
   400	(d) The condition $m\le\mbar$ suffices for the success proof, but not for the cost bound:
   401	$m=1$ on adversarial inputs would make $T'$ far too large. That is why one of the two rules
   402	in step~5 is mandatory.
   403	(e) An implementation (Python, gmpy2 and python-flint) is in
   404	\texttt{src/dcltheorem/algorithm.py}. Every candidate output passes a check $1<d<N$,
   405	$d\mid N$; an exhaustive regression test covers all $2\le N\le10^4$ for two parameter
   406	designs.
   407	\end{remark}
   408	
   409	%======================================================================
   410	\section{Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}}\label{sec:proofs}
   411	%======================================================================
   412	
   413	Throughout this section fix $(p,q)\in\Sn$, $N=pq$, a block $k$, and write $L=L_k$,
   414	$u=u_\ell$, $d=\ord_q(2)$. Since $p>P/2>16Y$ by \eqref{eq:scales}, every band prime is smaller
   415	than both factors.
   416	
   417	\subsection{Modulus lock, coverage and windows}
   418	
   419	\begin{lemma}[Modulus-squared lock; {\cite[Prop.~3.3]{hh2022}}]\label{lem:lock}
   420	Let $\ell\in\Pk$, $(A,B)\in V$, and let $r$ be a root of $Br^2\equiv AN\pmod\ell$. If
   421	$p\equiv r\pmod\ell$, then $g:=(Bp+Aq-c)/\ell^2\in\Z$.
   422	\end{lemma}
   423	\begin{proof}
   424	Since $\ell>Y>H\ge A,B$ and $\ell<p<q$, we have $\ell\nmid ABN$. Write $p=r+\ell x$. Modulo
   425	$\ell^2$, $p^{-1}\equiv r^{-1}(1-\ell xr^{-1})$, so $q=Np^{-1}\equiv s-\ell x\,sr^{-1}$. Hence
   426	$Bp+Aq\equiv c+\ell x(B-Asr^{-1})\pmod{\ell^2}$. Now $sr^{-1}\equiv Nr^{-2}\pmod\ell$ and
   427	$Br^2\equiv AN$, so $B-ANr^{-2}\equiv0\pmod\ell$, and the last term vanishes modulo
   428	$\ell^2$.
   429	\end{proof}
   430	
   431	\begin{lemma}[Coverage criterion]\label{lem:coverage}
   432	$p\bmod\ell$ is one of the computed roots if and only if $\ell\mid Bp-Aq$. In that case the
   433	Euler test in step~4 passes and the target $\tau=(v,\ell,p\bmod\ell)$ exists; we say that
   434	$\tau$ \emph{covers} $p$.
   435	\end{lemma}
   436	\begin{proof}
   437	$Bp^2-AN=p(Bp-Aq)$ and $\ell\nmid p$, so $Bp^2\equiv AN\pmod\ell$ iff $\ell\mid Bp-Aq$. If so,
   438	$a\equiv p^2\not\equiv0$ is a quadratic residue, and for $\ell\equiv3\pmod4$ the two roots
   439	are $\pm a^{(\ell+1)/4}$.
   440	\end{proof}
   441	
   442	\begin{lemma}[Window]\label{lem:window}
   443	If $\tau$ covers $p$, then $g_{\mathrm{lo}}\le g\le g_{\mathrm{hi}}$. Put $i_{\mathrm{full}}=g-g_{\mathrm{lo}}$,
   444	$j^*=\lfloor i_{\mathrm{full}}/m\rfloor$ and $i^*=i_{\mathrm{full}}-j^*m\in[0,m)$. Then
   445	$j^*<\lceil W_\tau/m\rceil$, so $G_{\tau,j^*}$ is one of the computed giants; we call it the
   446	\emph{true giant}.
   447	\end{lemma}
   448	\begin{proof}
   449	$p\in[a_0,b_0]$, so $\mathrm{Lo}_v\le F_v(p)=Bp+Aq\le\mathrm{Hi}_v$. Divide by $\ell^2$ after
   450	subtracting $c$. Finally $i_{\mathrm{full}}\le W_\tau-1$.
   451	\end{proof}
   452	
   453	\subsection{The collision modulo \texorpdfstring{$p$}{p} is exact}
   454	
   455	\begin{lemma}[Mod $p$]\label{lem:modp}
   456	If $\tau$ covers $p$, then $G_{\tau,j^*}\equiv\gamma^{i^*}\pmod p$. No hypothesis on any
   457	multiplicative order is needed.
   458	\end{lemma}
   459	\begin{proof}
   460	We have the identity
   461	\[
   462	  B+AN-(Bp+Aq)=(p-1)(Aq-B),
   463	\]
   464	so $B+AN-c=\ell^2g+(p-1)(Aq-B)$. Since $2^{p-1}\equiv1\pmod p$,
   465	$u^{B+AN-c}\equiv2^{(L/\ell^2)\ell^2g}=\gamma^g\pmod p$. Multiply by
   466	$\gamma^{-(g_{\mathrm{lo}}+j^*m)}$.
   467	\end{proof}
   468	
   469	\begin{lemma}[Mod $q$]\label{lem:modq}
   470	If $\tau$ covers $p$, then $G_{\tau,j^*}\equiv\gamma^{i^*}u^{(A-B)(p-1)}\pmod q$.
   471	\end{lemma}
   472	\begin{proof}
   473	Modulo $q-1$ we have $N\equiv p$, so $B+AN-c\equiv B+Ap-c$; also
   474	$\ell^2g=Bp+Aq-c\equiv Bp+A-c$. The difference is $(A-B)(p-1)$. Multiplying by $L/\ell^2$
   475	preserves the congruence modulo $q-1$, and $2^{q-1}\equiv1\pmod q$.
   476	\end{proof}
   477	
   478	\begin{definition}
   479	The true giant $G=G_{\tau,j^*}$ is \emph{FULL} if $G\equiv\gamma^{i'}\pmod q$ for some
   480	$i'\in[0,m)$.
   481	\end{definition}
   482	
   483	\begin{lemma}[One-sided extraction]\label{lem:extract}
   484	If $\tau$ covers $p$ and its true giant $G$ is not FULL, then $\gcd(f(G),N)=p$.
   485	\end{lemma}
   486	\begin{proof}
   487	$f(G)=\prod_{i<m}(G-\gamma^i)$. The factor $i=i^*$ vanishes modulo $p$ by
   488	Lemma~\ref{lem:modp}. Modulo $q$ no factor vanishes, by definition, and $\Z/q\Z$ is a field.
   489	\end{proof}
   490	
   491	\subsection{The clean band and the FULL criterion}
   492	
   493	\begin{lemma}[Clean band]\label{lem:clean}
   494	Call block $k$ \emph{clean for $q$} if no $\ell\in\Pk$ divides $q-1$. At least one of the four
   495	blocks is clean for $q$.
   496	\end{lemma}
   497	\begin{proof}
   498	Every band prime exceeds $Y$. If all four blocks were unclean, $q-1$ would have four
   499	distinct prime factors exceeding $Y$, so $q-1>Y^4>\sqrt2P>q$ by \eqref{eq:scales}, a
   500	contradiction.
   501	\end{proof}
   502	
   503	\begin{lemma}[FULL on a clean block]\label{lem:full}
   504	If block $k$ is clean for $q$, then the true giant is FULL if and only if
   505	\[
   506	  d\mid(A-B)(p-1)-\ell^2\delta\quad\text{for some }\delta\in[-i^*,\,m-1-i^*]\subset(-m,m).
   507	\]
   508	\end{lemma}
   509	\begin{proof}
   510	By Lemma~\ref{lem:modq}, FULL holds iff $u^{(A-B)(p-1)}\equiv\gamma^{\delta}\pmod q$ for some
   511	$\delta=i'-i^*$, i.e.\ iff $2^{(L/\ell^2)[(A-B)(p-1)-\ell^2\delta]}\equiv1\pmod q$. Since $d\mid q-1$
   512	and $\gcd(L,q-1)=1$ on a clean block, $\gcd(d,L/\ell^2)=1$, and the factor $L/\ell^2$ can be
   513	cancelled.
   514	\end{proof}
   515	
   516	The exact asymmetric range of $\delta$ matters for the equivalence; below we only use the
   517	enlarged range $|\delta|<\mbar$ as a superset for counting.
   518	
   519	\subsection{Three counting lemmas}
   520	
   521	\begin{lemma}[Small orders are rare]\label{lem:smallorder}
   522	For $Q_0\ge2$ and $T\ge1$,
   523	\[
   524	  \#\{q>Q_0\ \text{prime}:\ \ord_q(2)\le T\}\le\frac{T(T+1)}{2\log_2Q_0}.
   525	\]
   526	\end{lemma}
   527	\begin{proof}
   528	Such a $q$ divides $2^j-1$ for some $j\le T$. Since $2^j-1<2^j$, it has fewer than
   529	$j/\log_2Q_0$ prime factors exceeding $Q_0$. Sum over $j\le T$.
   530	\end{proof}
   531	
   532	\begin{lemma}[Coverage sieve]\label{lem:sieve}
   533	Fix $q$ and $k$. Let $U_k(q)$ be the set of integers $p\in[P/2,P]$ such that
   534	$\ell\nmid Bp-Aq$ for all $(v,\ell)\in V\times\Pk$. For $n\ge n_0$,
   535	\[
   536	  |U_k(q)|\le\frac{P/2+1+4^{k+1}Y^2}{J_k},\qquad J_k\ge|V|\sum_{\ell\in\Pk}\frac1\ell\ge\frac{c_1H^2}{n}.
   537	\]
   538	\end{lemma}
   539	\begin{proof}
   540	For $\ell\in\Pk$ we have $\ell\nmid q$, so $\ell\mid Bp-Aq$ iff $p\equiv AqB^{-1}\pmod\ell$. These
   541	$|V|$ classes are distinct: $AqB^{-1}\equiv A'qB'^{-1}$ would give $\ell\mid AB'-A'B$, but
   542	$0<|AB'-A'B|<H^2<Y<\ell$. So $U=U_k(q)$ avoids $\omega:=|V|<\ell$ residue classes modulo
   543	every $\ell\in\Pk$.
   544	
   545	Let $S(\alpha)=\sum_{u\in U}e(u\alpha)$ and $Z(h)=\#\{u\in U:u\equiv h\pmod\ell\}$. Then
   546	$\sum_{a=0}^{\ell-1}|S(a/\ell)|^2=\ell\sum_hZ(h)^2\ge\ell|U|^2/(\ell-\omega)$ by Cauchy--Schwarz,
   547	since $Z$ is supported on $\ell-\omega$ classes. Removing $a=0$,
   548	\[
   549	  \sum_{a=1}^{\ell-1}|S(a/\ell)|^2\ge\frac{\omega}{\ell-\omega}|U|^2.
   550	\]
   551	The points $a/\ell$ with $\ell\in\Pk$, $1\le a<\ell$, are $Q^{-2}$-separated modulo $1$ with
   552	$Q=2^{k+1}Y$, and $U$ lies in an interval of at most $N_0=P/2+1$ integers. The additive
   553	large sieve inequality \cite{mv1973} gives
   554	$\sum_{\ell,a}|S(a/\ell)|^2\le(N_0+Q^2)|U|$. Combining,
   555	$|U|\sum_{\ell\in\Pk}\omega/(\ell-\omega)\le N_0+Q^2$, and $\omega/(\ell-\omega)\ge|V|/\ell$.
   556	
   557	By the prime number theorem in the progression $3\bmod4$ (effective, the modulus being
   558	fixed), $\sum_{\ell\in\Pk}1/\ell\ge c'/n$, and $|V|\ge H^2/2$ since coprime pairs have density
   559	$6/\pi^2$.
   560	\end{proof}
   561	
   562	\begin{lemma}[FULL is rare]\label{lem:crt}
   563	Fix $q$ with $d=\ord_q(2)>T$ and a block $k$ clean for $q$. Let $F_k(q)$ be the set of
   564	integers $p\in[P/2,P]$ for which some $(v,\ell)\in V\times\Pk$ has $\ell\mid Bp-Aq$ and
   565	$d\mid(A-B)(p-1)-\ell^2\delta$ for some $|\delta|<\mbar$. Then
   566	\[
   567	  |F_k(q)|\le2|V||\Pk|\,\mbar\Bigl(\frac{PH}{2^{k+1}YT}+1\Bigr).
   568	\]
   569	\end{lemma}
   570	\begin{proof}
   571	Fix $(v,\ell,\delta)$ and let $g'=\gcd(A-B,d)\le H$ (recall $A\ne B$ and $|A-B|<H$). The
   572	congruence $(A-B)(p-1)\equiv\ell^2\delta\pmod d$ is either unsolvable or holds on exactly
   573	one class of $p$ modulo $d/g'$. Cleanliness gives $\ell\nmid q-1$, hence $\ell\nmid d$, so
   574	together with $p\equiv AqB^{-1}\pmod\ell$ the CRT gives one class modulo $\ell d/g'\ge\ell T/H$.
   575	An interval of length $P/2$ contains at most $PH/(2\ell T)+1$ of its elements, and
   576	$\ell>2^kY$. There are at most $|V||\Pk|(2\mbar-1)$ triples.
   577	\end{proof}
   578	
   579	\subsection{The exceptional set}
   580	Let $T=\lfloor2^{(1/4-\varepsilon)n}\rfloor$. For $(p,q)\in\Sn$ let $k(q)$ be the least block
   581	that is clean for $q$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:clean}).
   582	
   583	\begin{proposition}[Success certificate]\label{prop:cert}
   584	If (a) $d=\ord_q(2)>T$, (b) $p\notin U_{k(q)}(q)$, and (c) $p\notin F_{k(q)}(q)$, then
   585	$\DCL$ outputs a proper divisor of $N$.
   586	\end{proposition}
   587	\begin{proof}
   588	By (b) and Lemma~\ref{lem:coverage}, some target $\tau$ of block $k(q)$ covers $p$; by
   589	Lemma~\ref{lem:window} its true giant is computed. By (c) and Lemma~\ref{lem:full} it is
   590	not FULL, because the exact $\delta$-range lies inside $|\delta|<m\le\mbar$. By
   591	Lemma~\ref{lem:extract} its gcd is $p$, so block $k(q)$ outputs a proper divisor unless an
   592	earlier block already did. Either output is a proper divisor; it may be $q$ rather than
   593	$p$.
   594	\end{proof}
   595	
   596	Hence the \texttt{FAIL} pairs lie in $E_a\cup E_b\cup E_c$, where
   597	$E_a=\{d\le T\}$, $E_b=\{d>T,\ p\in U_{k(q)}(q)\}$, $E_c=\{d>T,\ p\in F_{k(q)}(q)\}$. We
   598	sum over the at most $3P/n$ primes $q\in(P/\sqrt2,\sqrt2P)$ and divide by $|\Sn|\ge c_0P^2/n^2$.
   599	This normalisation (one fixed interval for $p$, integer survivors counted, then summed over
   600	prime $q$) needs no prime counts in short intervals.
   601	
   602	\paragraph{$E_a$.} With $Q_0=2^{(n-1)/2}<q$, Lemma~\ref{lem:smallorder} bounds the bad $q$
   603	by $T(T+1)/(n-1)$, each with at most $P/2+1$ partners. So
   604	$|E_a|\le(1+o(1))2^{(1-2\varepsilon)n}/n$ and $|E_a|/|\Sn|\le(n/c_0)2^{-2\varepsilon n}(1+o(1))$.
   605	
   606	\paragraph{$E_b$.} By Lemma~\ref{lem:sieve} and $Y^2=o(P)$,
   607	$\sum_q|U_{k(q)}(q)|\le(3P/n)\cdot(P/2+1+256Y^2)\cdot n/(c_1H^2)\le CP^2/H^2$. So
   608	$|E_b|/|\Sn|\le Cn^2/H^2\le C2^{-\varepsilon n}$.
   609	
   610	\paragraph{$E_c$.} Use $|V|\le H^2$, $|\Pk|\le2^kY$ and $\mbar\le3HP/(4^kY^2)$ in
   611	Lemma~\ref{lem:crt}: $|F_k(q)|\le6H^4P^2/(Y^2T)+6H^3P/Y$. Summing over $q$ and using
   612	$Y^2\ge P^{2/3}H^{-2/3}$,
   613	\[
   614	  \frac{|E_c|}{|\Sn|}\le\frac{3P}{n}\Bigl(\frac{6H^4P^2}{Y^2T}+\frac{6H^3P}{Y}\Bigr)\frac{n^2}{c_0P^2}
   615	  \le Cn\Bigl(\frac{H^{14/3}P^{1/3}}{T}+\frac{H^{10/3}}{P^{1/3}}\Bigr).
   616	\]
   617	With $H\le2n2^{\varepsilon n/2}$, $P=2^{n/2}$ and $T\asymp2^{(1/4-\varepsilon)n}$, the two terms are
   618	at most $n^{O(1)}2^{(-1/12+10\varepsilon/3)n}$ and $n^{O(1)}2^{(-1/6+5\varepsilon/3)n}$. For
   619	$\varepsilon\le1/60$ the first exponent is at most $-n/36$, which beats $2^{-\varepsilon n}=2^{-n/60}$ by
   620	$2^{-n/90}$, enough to absorb the polynomial factors. (The requirement is
   621	$-1/12+13\varepsilon/3<0$, i.e.\ $\varepsilon<1/52$.)
   622	
   623	\paragraph{Conclusion.} The total exceptional fraction is $O(2^{-\varepsilon n})=O(N^{-\varepsilon})$,
   624	which proves part (iii). Part (i) holds because only gcds in $(1,N)$ and band primes
   625	$1<\ell<N$ dividing $N$ are output, and inputs $N<4$ return \texttt{FAIL}. \qed
   626	
   627	\begin{remark}[Where the constants come from]
   628	The bound $\varepsilon\le1/60$ is used only in $E_c$ (which needs $\varepsilon<1/52$) and in the scale
   629	separations \eqref{eq:scales} ($\varepsilon<1/7$ and $\varepsilon<1/4$). The binding term is $E_b$: the
   630	large sieve gives $n^2/H^2$, so an exceptional fraction $N^{-\varepsilon}$ costs $H=N^{\varepsilon/2}$,
   631	i.e.\ a factor $N^{5\varepsilon/6}$ in the running time. A Poisson heuristic predicts an
   632	uncovered fraction $e^{-\lambda}$ with $\lambda\asymp H^2/n$, which would give $N^{-\varepsilon}$ already
   633	at polylogarithmic $H$ (conjectured; Section~\ref{sec:open}).
   634	\end{remark}
   635	
   636	\subsection{Bit complexity}\label{sec:cost}
   637	We assume quasi-linear integer multiplication $M(b)=O(b\log b)$ \cite{hvdh2021}
   638	(Sch\"onhage--Strassen costs an extra $\log\log$). Per block, with $T_k$ targets and
   639	$m\le\mbar$:
   640	
   641	\begin{center}
   642	\small
   643	\begin{tabular}{p{0.42\textwidth}p{0.22\textwidth}p{0.24\textwidth}}
   644	\toprule
   645	step & operations & bit cost\\
   646	\midrule
   647	prime sieve to $16Y$; $V$; $\mathrm{Lo}_v,\mathrm{Hi}_v$ & $O(Y\log\log Y)$; $O(H^2)$ square roots & $\tilde O(Y+H^2n)$\\
   648	integer product tree of $\{\ell^2\}$ ($\log_2L_k\le3\cdot2^kY$) & balanced tree & $O(M(Y)\log Y)$\\
   649	exponent tree; $\gamma,\gamma^{-1}$ & $O(Y\log Y)$ mults mod $N$, 1 inversion & $O(Y\log Y\,M(n))$\\
   650	roots, $s$, $c$, windows & $O(|V||\Pk|\log Y)$ mults mod $\ell,\ell^2$ & $\tilde O(H^2Y)$\\
   651	$w_\ell=u_\ell^N$; giants & $O(n|\Pk|+nT_k+T')$ mults mod $N$ & $O((n(T_k+Y)+T')M(n))$\\
   652	choice of $m$; small inversions and reductions & $O(nT_k)$ small-integer ops & $\tilde O(T_kn^{O(1)})$\\
   653	babies $\gamma^i$ & $m$ mults & $O(mM(n))$\\
   654	$f$ by product tree; remainder tree at $T'$ giants & $O((M(m)+M(T'))\log(m+T'))$ ring ops & $\tilde O((m+T')n)$\\
   655	gcds & $T'$ gcds of $n$-bit integers & $O(T'M(n)\log n)$\\
   656	\bottomrule
   657	\end{tabular}
   658	\end{center}
   659	
   660	Polynomial products over $\Z/N\Z$ use Kronecker substitution with slots wider than
   661	$2n+\lceil\log_2(D+1)\rceil$, so a degree-$D$ product costs $O(M(D(2n+\log D)))$ bit
   662	operations \cite{vzgg}. Remainders by monic polynomials use reversal and Newton inversion of
   663	a power series with constant term $1$, so no inverse of a nonunit is ever needed.
   664	
   665	By step~5, $m+T'\le\mbar+T_k\le\tfrac52HP/Y^2+4+2H^2\cdot2^{k+1}Y$. Over the four blocks the
   666	total is
   667	\[
   668	  O\bigl(n^{O(1)}(HP/Y^2+H^2Y)\bigr)=O\bigl(n^{O(1)}H^{5/3}P^{1/3}\bigr)
   669	  =O\bigl(n^{O(1)}2^{(1/6+5\varepsilon/6)n}\bigr),
   670	\]
   671	because $Y\asymp P^{1/3}H^{-1/3}$. This proves part (ii). \qed
   672	
   673	\subsection{Proof of Corollary~\ref{cor:polylog}}
   674	Take $H=\lceil n^{1+a}\rceil$, $Y$ as before, and in the analysis $T=\lfloor2^{n/4}n^{-1-a}\rfloor$. The
   675	scale separations \eqref{eq:scales} hold for large $n$. The three exceptional terms become
   676	$O(n^{-1-2a})$ ($E_a$), $O(n^{-2a})$ ($E_b$) and
   677	$n^{O(1)}\cdot2^{-n/12}\cdot n^{O(1+a)}=o(n^{-2a})$ ($E_c$). The cost is
   678	$n^{O(1)}H^{5/3}2^{n/6}=N^{1/6}(\log N)^{O(1+a)}$. \qed
   679	
   680	%======================================================================
   681	\section{The \texorpdfstring{$1/6$}{1/6} floor in the collision-search model}\label{sec:lower}
   682	%======================================================================
   683	
   684	This section is context. Its results were proved in the course of the project
   685	(\texttt{notes/cycloharvey\_results.md} \S3, \texttt{notes/multimod\_results.md} \S3) and
   686	checked by one independent AI critic and by the GPT-6.1-sol round-12 referee, who judged
   687	Theorems~\ref{thm:A}, \ref{thm:Aprime} and~\ref{thm:C} correct in the stipulated model and
   688	Theorem~\ref{thm:B} correct given a short counting argument, which we include. We give the
   689	statements and the main steps.
   690	
   691	\subsection{The model}
   692	Write $P=\sqrt N$ and $t=p/P\in J=[c^{-1/2},1]$ for a fixed $c>1$. A \emph{target} $\tau$
   693	consists of
   694	\begin{itemize}
   695	\item a non-constant exponent map $g_\tau(x)=\sum_{k\in I,\,k>0}\alpha_kx^{k}+\sum_{k\in I,\,k<0}\alpha_k(N/x)^{|k|}$,
   696	  i.e.\ a polynomial in $p$ and $q=N/p$ without constant term, with support
   697	  $I\subseteq[-d,d]\setminus\{0\}$, $|I|=K$, and coefficients whose denominators involve only
   698	  primes dividing $M_\tau$;
   699	\item a modulus $M_\tau\ge1$ with $\gcd(M_\tau,N)=1$, a residue class $r_\tau$ and a constant
   700	  $c_\tau$, such that $g_\tau(x)-c_\tau$ is integral on the whole class: for each prime
   701	  $\ell\mid M_\tau$ it is $\ell$-adically integral for every $x\in\Z_\ell$ with
   702	  $x\equiv r_\tau\pmod{\ell^{v_\ell(M_\tau)}}$ ($N$ is an $\ell$-adic unit). The condition is
   703	  imposed on the whole residue class, as an identity; requiring it only at the actual
   704	  divisors of $N$ would make the model vacuous;
   705	\item an integer window $[U_\tau,U_\tau+W_\tau)$.
   706	\end{itemize}
   707	A family \emph{covers} if every integer $p\in[\sqrt{N/c},\sqrt N]$ satisfies
   708	$p\equiv r_\tau\pmod{M_\tau}$ and $g_\tau(p)\in[U_\tau,U_\tau+W_\tau)$ for some $\tau$. Its
   709	\emph{cost} is any $X$ with
   710	\[
   711	  \#\{\text{distinct }(M_\tau,r_\tau,g_\tau)\}\le X\quad\text{and}\quad\sum_\tau W_\tau\le X^2.
   712	\]
   713	These hypotheses hold for one shared baby list, per-modulus lists, or any mix, since
   714	$\sum_\tau W_\tau\le\sum_\tau m\lceil W_\tau/m\rceil\le X\max m\le X^2$. Call this model $\mathfrak M^+$.
   715	With a single modulus it is the identity model in which Lehman, Hittmeir, Harvey and
   716	Harvey--Hittmeir argue; $\DCL$ fits in it with $M_\tau=\ell$, $g_\tau=(Bp+Aq-c)/\ell^2$ and
   717	$W_\tau\approx HP/\ell^2$.
   718	
   719	\subsection{Per-target coverage}
   720	With $p=Pt$ and $q=N/p=P/t$, write $g_\tau=G(t)=\sum_{k\in I}b_kt^k$ with
   721	$b_k=\alpha_kP^{|k|}$.
   722	
   723	\begin{lemma}[Coverage of one target]\label{lem:C}
   724	For any measurable $E\subset\R$,
   725	$|\{t\in J:G(t)\in E\}|\le C_I(|E|/\|b\|_\infty)^{1/K}$.
   726	\end{lemma}
   727	\noindent\emph{Sketch.} The generalised Wronskian $\det[(i)_k]_{k\le K,\,i\in I}$ is non-zero
   728	for $0\notin I$, so on $J$ some derivative $|G^{(k)}|$, $k\le K$, is $\ge c_I\|b\|_\infty$ on each
   729	of $O_I(1)$ pieces; apply the van der Corput sublevel-set estimate on each piece.
   730	
   731	\begin{lemma}[Denominators]\label{lem:K}
   732	If $g_\tau$ is integer-valued up to a constant on a class modulo $M$, then
   733	$C_dM^K\alpha\in\Z^K$, with $C_d$ depending only on $d$.
   734	\end{lemma}
   735	
   736	From these two lemmas: for degree $e=1$ and $K=2$, writing $g=(Aq+Bp-c)/(C_dM^2)$ with
   737	$H=\max(|A|,|B|)$, the number of integers covered by one target is at most
   738	\begin{equation}\label{eq:pertarget}
   739	  C(PW/H)^{1/2}+C,
   740	\end{equation}
   741	and the modulus $M$ cancels exactly (the per-target form of ``guess-neutrality''). For
   742	$e\ge2$ the bound is $CP^{1-e/K}W^{1/K}+C$, again independent of $M$. Equality in
   743	\eqref{eq:pertarget} with $H=O(1)$ and $W\approx P/M^2$ means that a target covers its whole
   744	residue class: exactly what the targets of $\DCL$ do.
   745	
   746	\subsection{Statements}
   747	
   748	\begin{theorem}[Theorem A; proved in the model]\label{thm:A}
   749	For fixed $d$ and $c>1$, every covering family in $\mathfrak M^+$ of degree at most $d$ has cost
   750	$X\ge c_{d,c}N^{1/6}$.
   751	\end{theorem}
   752	\begin{proof}
   753	The $e=1$ targets cover at most
   754	$CP^{1/2}\sum_\tau W_\tau^{1/2}+CX\le CP^{1/2}X^{1/2}(\sum W_\tau)^{1/2}+CX\le CP^{1/2}X^{3/2}+CX$
   755	integers, by \eqref{eq:pertarget} and Cauchy--Schwarz. By H\"older over the finitely many
   756	classes $(e,K)$, the $e\ge2$ targets cover at most $C\sum_{(e,K)}P^{1-e/K}X^{1+1/K}+CX$. If
   757	$X=\delta P^{2/5}$ with $\delta$ small, each $(e,K)$ term is at most $C\delta P$, because $5e\ge2K+2$
   758	when $K\le2e$ and $e\ge2$. So the $e=1$ part must cover a positive proportion of the $\asymp P$
   759	candidates, which forces $X\ge cP^{1/3}=cN^{1/6}$. Otherwise $X>\delta P^{2/5}\ge N^{1/6}$.
   760	\end{proof}
   761	
   762	\begin{theorem}[Theorem A$'$; proved in the model]\label{thm:Aprime}
   763	If the degree-one targets of a covering family cover fewer than half of the candidates,
   764	then $X\ge cN^{1/5}$, whatever the moduli. In particular, targets of degree $\ge2$ never
   765	beat $N^{1/5}$.
   766	\end{theorem}
   767	
   768	\begin{theorem}[Theorem B; proved in the model]\label{thm:B}
   769	If the degree-one targets use $J$ distinct moduli, all at most $P$, then
   770	$X\ge cN^{1/5}(JN^{o(1)})^{-1/5}$. Exponent $1/5-\eta$ therefore needs $J\ge N^{5\eta-o(1)}$
   771	moduli.
   772	\end{theorem}
   773	\begin{proof}[Main step]
   774	For a modulus $M$ and weights $(A,B)$, the classes on which $(Aq+Bp-c)/(C_dM^2)$ is
   775	integer-valued satisfy $Br^2\equiv AN$ modulo a divisor of $M$, so they number at most
   776	$O(1)^{\omega(M)}\gcd(A,B,M)$. Using $\gcd(A,B,M)=\sum_{e\mid\gcd(A,B,M)}\varphi(e)$, the number of
   777	(class, weight) pairs with height at most $h$ is $\ll h^2\sum_{e\mid M}\varphi(e)/e^2\le h^2\tau(M)=
   778	h^2M^{o(1)}$. Over $J$ moduli this is at most $J'h^2$ with $J'=JN^{o(1)}$, so the $X$ smallest
   779	heights satisfy $\sum_\tau1/H_\tau\le C(J'X)^{1/2}$. Cauchy--Schwarz on \eqref{eq:pertarget} bounds
   780	the degree-one coverage by $CP^{1/2}X(J'X)^{1/4}+CX$; requiring this to be $\gg P$ gives the
   781	claim. Single-term targets and $e\ge2$ are handled as in Theorem~\ref{thm:A}.
   782	\end{proof}
   783	
   784	\begin{theorem}[Theorem C: CRT is multiplicative; proved]\label{thm:C}
   785	Let $M=M_1M_2$ with $\gcd(M_1,M_2)=1$, let $L_{r,M}$ be the lattice of support-$I$ Laurent
   786	polynomials that are integer-valued up to a constant on the class $r\bmod M$, and $\Lambda_I$
   787	the integer-coefficient lattice. Then $L_{r,M}=L_{r_1,M_1}+L_{r_2,M_2}$ and
   788	$[L_{r,M}:\Lambda_I]=[L_{r_1,M_1}:\Lambda_I]\,[L_{r_2,M_2}:\Lambda_I]$.
   789	\end{theorem}
   790	
   791	\begin{remark}[Interpretation]
   792	Theorem~\ref{thm:C} says that a scheme covering \emph{all} classes of a composite modulus
   793	gains nothing from its CRT structure (heuristically, via a volume argument; the rigorous
   794	single-modulus statement is the $J=1$ case of Theorem~\ref{thm:B}). What escapes the
   795	$N^{1/5}$ bound is \emph{selective} covering: each $p$ needs only one modulus whose class is
   796	reached by a short weight vector. $\DCL$ uses only the $\le2$ classes per $(v,\ell)$ that
   797	have a root, a $2/\ell$ fraction, but each is reached by a vector of height $O(H)$ instead of
   798	the typical $\sqrt\ell$. Across $N^{1/6+o(1)}$ coprime moduli these classes cover almost
   799	everything (Theorem~\ref{thm:main}), and conjecturally everything
   800	(Conjecture~\ref{conj:DCH}).
   801	\end{remark}
   802	
   803	\begin{remark}[Quantifiers]
   804	Theorems~\ref{thm:A}--\ref{thm:B} bound families that cover \emph{every} integer candidate for
   805	a fixed $N$. Theorem~\ref{thm:main} bounds the failure at the \emph{true} factor, averaged over
   806	$\Sn$. So Theorem~\ref{thm:main} does not show that the model exponent is $1/6$ for almost
   807	every $N$; it shows that the configuration that is extremal for Theorem~\ref{thm:A} is
   808	attained at the true factor for almost every pair. Going below $1/6$ needs a non-sumset
   809	collision mechanism (and, for full factoring, small-factor removal faster than
   810	Pollard--Strassen, which costs $N^{1/6+o(1)}$ for factors up to $N^{1/3}$).
   811	\end{remark}
   812	
