   935	%======================================================================
   936	\section{Limitations and open problems}\label{sec:open}
   937	%======================================================================
   938	
   939	\paragraph{Worst case.} Theorem~\ref{thm:main} is not a worst-case result. Three independent
   940	obstacles remain.
   941	\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
   942	\item \emph{Coverage of every candidate.} This would follow from the following conjecture.
   943	\begin{conjecture}[Divisor-cover hypothesis DCH$(\kappa)$]\label{conj:DCH}
   944	For all large $N$ coprime to $6$ and every integer $p\in[\sqrt{N/2},\sqrt N]$ there are coprime
   945	$A,B\le(\log N)^\kappa$ and a prime $\ell\in(N^{1/6},N^{1/6}(\log N)^\kappa]$ with $\ell\mid Bp^2-AN$.
   946	\end{conjecture}
   947	It is \emph{experimentally supported} only at 32--48 bits (zero uncovered candidates among
   948	$1.6\cdot10^4$--$4.9\cdot10^6$ per instance, 25 instances, at $\lambda\approx\ln\sqrt N+2$). The large sieve
   949	gives only ``almost all''.
   950	\item \emph{Primes $q$ with small $\ord_q(2)$} (at most $N^{1/4-\varepsilon}$). The proof discards them.
   951	A worst-case version needs a base of large order modulo the specific $q$, on a clean band;
   952	large order modulo $N$ \cite{hh2026} does not give that.
   953	\item \emph{Pairs whose every covering true giant is FULL.} They are rare on average but not
   954	excluded for a fixed $N$.
   955	\end{enumerate}
   956	
   957	\paragraph{Unbalanced ratios.} Only $q/p\le2$ is covered. For $q/p\approx R$ the weights need
   958	$B/A\approx R$ and the window grows to $\approx\sqrt{RN}$; with a shared list a band costs about
   959	$R^{1/4}N^{1/6}$, and summing dyadic ratios up to $N^{1/3}$ gives worse than Harvey's $1/5$
   960	(heuristic; \texttt{notes/multimod\_results.md} \S3.9). A ratio-uniform version is open.
   961	Extending to $q/p\le N^{o(1)}$ seems plausible but is not done here.
   962	
   963	\paragraph{Exceptional rate.} The binding step is the large sieve's $n^2/H^2$. The Poisson
   964	heuristic predicts $e^{-cH^2/n}$, which would give $N^{-\varepsilon}$ exceptions at polylogarithmic
   965	cost (\emph{conjectured}). For a fixed $q$ the $p$-interval has length $P\approx Y^3$, so a sieve
   966	whose level is limited to about $P$ sees only moduli built from at most three band primes.
   967	We \emph{expect} (heuristic, not a theorem) that one-variable upper-bound sieves at such levels
   968	give only polynomial savings in $1/\lambda$. We have not proved any barrier: averaging over $q$
   969	gives a two-variable problem over the box with level up to $P^2$, where bilinear or
   970	dispersion methods might do better.
   971	
   972	\paragraph{Below $1/6$.} Theorem~\ref{thm:A} rules this out for one-base sumset
   973	collision search with polynomial exponent maps, whatever the moduli. Escaping needs a
   974	different collision mechanism (multi-base searches with non-split known relations are not
   975	covered by the reduction in the project notes), together with small-factor removal below
   976	$N^{1/6}$.
   977	
   978	\paragraph{Practice.} At 40--80 bits the algorithm is $2.3$--$4.9$ times more expensive than
   979	Harvey--Hittmeir in our cost proxy. The asymptotic advantage, if real at larger sizes, is
   980	in the exponent only. The heuristically subexponential methods (ECM, quadratic and number
   981	field sieves) are of course far faster; this work concerns only rigorous deterministic
   982	bounds.
