   395	## 6. Where the result stops (precise remaining gaps)
   396	
   397	1. **Worst case (every N in S_n) is NOT proved.** Three independent obstacles remain:
   398	   - (a) **coverage** of every p, which is multimod's DCH (conjectured; the large sieve gives
   399	     only "almost all");
   400	   - (b) **q with small ord_q(2)** (≤ N^{1/4−ε}). The proof simply discards them (E_a). A
   401	     worst-case version would need a base with large order mod the specific prime q *on a
   402	     clean band*. Harvey–Hittmeir 2026 gives a large-order element mod N, but large order
   403	     mod N does not give large order mod q;
   404	   - (c) pairs where **every** covering true giant is FULL (E_c). These are rare on average,
   405	     but not excluded for a fixed N.
   406	2. **The exceptional rate is limited by the large sieve.** The proved fraction is n²/H², so
   407	   N^{−ε} costs H = N^{ε/2}, i.e. N^{5ε/6} in the cost. The Poisson heuristic, which the
   408	   multimod and §8 data support, predicts e^{−λ} with λ ≍ H²/n. That would give N^{−ε} at
   409	   polylog cost, but it is **conjectured**.
   410	3. **Ratios q/p > 2** are not covered (multimod §3.9).
   411	4. **Finite sizes.** At 40–80 bits HH is 2.3–4.9× cheaper in E (§8). The measured slope is
   412	   smaller (0.203 vs 0.226), and an extrapolated crossover lies near 130 bits. That is **not a
   413	   claim**: the slope difference partly reflects the H-schedule.
   414	5. **Below 1/6.** multimod Thm A (enlarged sumset model) is unchanged. Nothing here goes
   415	   below 1/6.
