   333	failure fraction O(1/log² N).
   334	
   335	Remarks.
   336	- Only the search is covered. Success also needs γ = β^L to have order mod p (or mod N) larger
   337	  than the exponent range compared, about m + W, or one-sided detection otherwise (§3.8).
   338	- For a *fixed* N, the same sieve in p (classes = roots of Br² ≡ AN) bounds the uncovered
   339	  candidates by ≈ #cand/J_N. But J_N involves Σ_v (ABN/ℓ), and lower-bounding it needs
   340	  quadratic-character cancellation over the short range ℓ ≈ N^{1/6}. That is UNVERIFIED in
   341	  general; it would follow on average over ℓ from Heath-Brown's quadratic large sieve.
   342	  Either way it gives "almost all candidates", not "all".
   343	
   344	### 3.6 Conjecture DCH (divisor-cover hypothesis) and the worst case
   345	
   346	**DCH(κ).** For all large N coprime to 6 and every integer p ∈ [√(N/2), √N], some coprime
   347	A, B ≤ (log N)^κ and some prime ℓ ∈ (N^{1/6}, N^{1/6}(log N)^κ] satisfy ℓ | Bp² − AN.
   348	
   349	- Under DCH, DCL is a deterministic covering in 𝔐⁺ with cost N^{1/6+o(1)}. With Thm A, the
   350	  𝔐⁺ exponent is then **exactly 1/6**.
   351	- **Status: conjectured.**
   352	  - Experimentally supported at 32–48 bits: zero uncovered candidates among all ≈10⁵–5·10⁶
   353	    integer candidates per instance at λ = ln√N + 2 (§6).
   354	  - The large sieve proves only the "almost all" version.
   355	- **Relation to Umans–Wang (codex check).** UW's Strong Prefactored Divisor Conjecture asks
   356	  for structured S, T whose differences hit multiples of every d ≤ n. DCH asks that each
   357	  *candidate* p make one of the forms Bp² − AN divisible by a prime from a window. Neither
   358	  implication was found, and both are "every element of a long range is hit by a
   359	  structured divisor family" statements.
   360	  - He–Sahai's obstruction concerns UW's single-AP version of a different covering
   361	    statement (every d ≤ n dividing a difference). DCH is a sieve statement: each (v, ℓ)
   362	    removes ρ ≤ 2 N-dependent quadratic residue classes mod a prime ℓ from a window
   363	    around N^{1/6}. We know no transfer of their argument to it, but we have not proved
   364	    that none exists.
   365	
   366	### 3.7 Theorem E: multi-base searches
   367	
   368	**Setting.** In 𝔐^k, let Λ_known ⊂ Z^k be the relations ∏α_i^{x_i} ≡ 1 provable mod N for
   369	all p, i.e. the identity-model relations. Coverage must hold in G = Z^k/Λ_known, because two
   370	collisions mod p differing by an unknown relation are not identity-provable.
   371	
   372	**Statement.**
   373	- (a) **Proved.** The sumset bound |B|·|G_τ| ≥ |image of box_τ in G| holds in any abelian
   374	  group. So Σ_τ |image| ≤ X² with a shared B, and #targets ≤ X.
   375	- (b) **Proved, trivially.** If all bases are known powers of one β (G ≅ Z), as with DCL's γ
   376	  and β^{L/M²}, the search is a 1-D search with target Σ e_i F_i ∈ L, and 𝔐⁺ applies.
   377	- (c) **Proved for independent bases (Λ_known = 0).**
   378	  - Each α_i^{F_i(p,q)} must be identity-valid on its own, so each non-constant F_i lies in
   379	    Λ ∪ L_{r,M}, and Lemma K′ applies to it.
   380	  - Every coordinate must lie in its window, so μ_τ ≤ min_i μ(F_i, W_i) ≤ μ(F_{i*}, vol_τ),
   381	    using W_i ≤ ∏_j W_j = vol_τ.
   382	  - With Σ vol_τ ≤ X² (box ⊆ B + G_τ, injective in Z^k), the coverage bounds (1)–(2) and
   383	    Thms A–B hold verbatim. The same holds when Λ_known splits the bases into groups, each
   384	    group a set of known powers of one generator: apply (b) inside each group and (c)
   385	    across the groups.
   386	- (d) **Not covered (GAP, critic finding 2).** General Λ_known ≠ 0 with free rank k′ ≥ 2 and
   387	  non-split relations.
   388	  - A single F_i need then only be valid modulo Λ_known, so Lemma K′ need not apply
   389	    coordinatewise.
   390	  - Projected windows are sums of APs rather than intervals, so Lemma C's per-interval
   391	    constant is not controlled.
   392	  - We know no mechanism that would exploit this, but the reduction is **not proved**
   393	    there.
   394	
   395	**Cross-difference.** A test is multipoint-evaluable with one product tree iff it is
   396	separable in G: E(τ, j; i) = f_τ(j) − h(i). Baby sets mixing σ-powers or bases with
   397	p-dependent coefficients are non-separable, as cycloharvey §3.5 showed. Splitting them
   398	into separable pieces gives back case (c).
   399	- **Verdict.** For independent or grouped bases, k-dimensional exponents do not help
   400	  beyond what several moduli already give: **refuted for this model**. General
   401	  known-relation lattices are open (d).
   402	
   403	### 3.8 Costs outside the search (not solved here)
   404	
   405	- **Order of γ = β^L.** ord_p(γ) = ord_p(β)/gcd(ord_p(β), L). It becomes small when p − 1 is
   406	  divisible by two or more of the moduli, which happens with probability
   407	  ≈ (Σ_{ℓ∈𝒫}1/ℓ)²/2 ≍ (log u/log Y)².
   408	  - Then the baby list repeats mod p. That reveals p by gcd (class PM1) unless it is
   409	    simultaneously small mod q.
   410	  - **The deterministic order lemma for L-th powers is open (UNVERIFIED).**
   411	    Oznovich–Volk give order ≥ D in D^{1/2+o(1)}, but for β, not β^L.
   412	- **Small factors.** Removing factors ≤ N^{1/3} by Pollard–Strassen costs N^{1/6+o(1)}
   413	  (r10). That is at the same exponent, so it is no obstruction to 1/6 but blocks going
   414	  lower.
   415	
   416	### 3.9 Unbalanced range (limitation; heuristic)
   417	
   418	For q/p ≈ R ≫ 1, the weights need B/A ≈ R, and F's span grows to ≈ √(RN).
   419	- With per-band baby lists the band costs ≈ (RN)^{1/6}. With the shared list it costs
   420	  R^{1/4}N^{1/6}.
   421	- Summing over dyadic R up to N^{1/3} gives N^{2/9} or N^{1/4}. That is worse than Harvey,
   422	  whose Lehman pairs ab ≤ r cover all ratios at once.
   423	- So DCL as specified is a balanced-range method. A uniform-ratio version is
   424	  **not yet tested**.
   425	
   426	## 4. The three orchestrator inputs
   427	
   428	- **Gray-code / Stage-3 leaves (user).**
   429	  - Agreed, and it is the content of Thm C. Any all-classes scheme keeps
   430	    #leaves ≍ φ(M)·m₀ = N^{1/5}; only the generation cost changes.
   431	  - DCL reduces the number of **distinct leaves** to N^{1/6+o(1)}.
   432	  - Each DCL leaf covers a whole residue class across the range, P/M ≈ (PW)^{1/2}
   433	    candidates, the maximum allowed by (1). An HH leaf covers about P^{1/3}W^{2/3}·N^{−o(1)}.
   434	- **r10 warnings.**
   435	  - (3) Separate bases: solved by γ = β^L (§2.3). The baby lists coincide as group elements.
   436	  - (4) Base generation: §3.8, open for L-th powers.
   437	  - (2) UW / He–Sahai: §3.6.
   438	- **Chirp / quadratic-in-k leaves (user).** Leaves Y_k = Y₀γ₁^kγ₂^{k²} cost O(1) to
   439	  generate, but Stage 3 is the product tree ∏_k(X − Y_k) evaluated at the baby points. Its
   440	  cost is Õ(#leaves + #babies) however the leaves were made, so cheap generation saves only
   441	  Stage 2.
   442	  - Could chirp leaves cover more exponents per leaf? No. The covered exponent set is
   443	    E = {e_k + j}, with |E| ≤ #leaves·#babies, so the sumset bound |B|·|G| ≥ W is unchanged.
   444	  - Each window W_k is set by the unknown p inside a bucket, (√(ap) − √(bq))², not by
   445	    centre truncation (orchestrator (ii)). So the number of leaves Σ_k⌈W_k/m⌉ is the same as
   446	    without the chirp.
   447	  - When leaves and babies share β, the double product over (leaves × babies) is literally
   448	    the 1-D test over E. The chirp is a Stage-2 constant, as you expected.
   449	  - **Refuted for this model** (as an exponent improvement).
   450	
   451	## 5. Prior art (GPT-6.1-sol check, 2026-10-18; curated)
   452	
   453	- **Ingredients (PROVED as prior art).**
   454	  - Lehman 1974: multipliers.
   455	  - McKee 1999: a modulus-squared restriction "if a prime m divides …", heuristic N^{1/4+ε}.
   456	  - Hittmeir 2018: modular-hyperbola sieving over many small primes.
   457	  - Harvey 2021: a shared collision search over multiplier families, and Alg. 4.1.
   458	  - HH 2022 Prop. 3.3: the class lock, with a single modulus and lattice-chosen weights.
   459	  - Hart 2012: one-line factoring, many multipliers, heuristic N^{1/3}.
   460	- **Not found (UNVERIFIED that none exists).** No source with the union over many prime
   461	  moduli near N^{1/6} with fixed small weights, nor with the cofactor-exponent shared baby
   462	  list. We claim nothing beyond "no prior formulation found by us".
   463	- **Context.** A heuristic N^{1/6} is not a general factoring milestone: ECM and QS are
   464	  conjecturally subexponential. The interest is only within the deterministic
   465	  collision-search line (Harvey/HH/UW).

