    37	The following supplies the missing normalization and makes the coverage statement precise.
    38	
    39	Put
    40	\[
    41	X=2^n,\qquad P=X^{1/2},\qquad H=H_0,\qquad
    42	Y=\left\lceil X^{1/6}H^{-1/3}\right\rceil.
    43	\]
    44	Define
    45	\[
    46	\mathcal S_n=\{pq:X/2\le pq<X,\ p<q\le2p,\ p,q\text{ prime}\}.
    47	\]
    48	
    49	The statement that is supported is
    50	\[
    51	\frac{\#\{pq\in\mathcal S_n:p\text{ is structurally uncovered}\}}
    52	{|\mathcal S_n|}
    53	\ll_\varepsilon X^{-\varepsilon}.
    54	\]
    55	
    56	This is uniform counting over semiprimes in that set. Unique factorization identifies it with counting unordered prime pairs. It is not an assertion for every \(q\), every fixed \(N\), or an arbitrary prime-generation distribution.
    57	
    58	Every admissible smaller factor lies in the **single fixed interval**
    59	\[
    60	P/2\le p\le P.
    61	\]
    62	Every admissible \(q\) is of order \(P\), and there are \(O(P/\log P)\) possible prime \(q\)'s. Thus there is no need to normalize separately inside the potentially short \(I_q\).
    63	
    64	For each fixed \(q\), let
    65	\[
    66	\Omega_\ell(q)=
    67	\{AqB^{-1}\pmod\ell:(A,B)\in V\}.
    68	\]
    69	For sufficiently large \(n\), \(\ell>2H^2\), \(\ell\nmid q\), and
    70	\[
    71	|\Omega_\ell(q)|=|V|\asymp H^2.
    72	\]
    73	
    74	The arithmetic large-sieve bound is
    75	\[
    76	Z_q\le \frac{O(P)+Q^2}{G(Q)},\qquad Q=2Y,
    77	\]
    78	where
    79	\[
    80	G(Q)=\sum_{d\le Q}\mu^2(d)
    81	 \prod_{\ell\mid d}\frac{\omega(\ell)}{\ell-\omega(\ell)}.
    82	\]
    83	Only the band primes are active. Its prime terms already give
    84	\[
    85	G(Q)\ge\sum_{Y<\ell\le2Y}
    86	\frac{|V|}{\ell-|V|}
    87	\gg\frac{H^2}{\log Y}.
    88	\]
    89	This is the standard arithmetic consequence of the additive large sieve. [Montgomery–Vaughan, *The large sieve*](https://personal.science.psu.edu/rcv4/personal/Publications/large_sieve.pdf).
    90	
    91	Consequently,
    92	\[
    93	Z_q\ll \frac{(P+Y^2)\log P}{H^2}.
    94	\]
    95	This counts **all integer survivors**, so also bounds prime survivors. Summing over prime \(q\),
    96	\[
    97	E_n\ll\frac{P^2+PY^2}{H^2}.
    98	\]
    99	Ordinary prime number theory in fixed proportional intervals gives
   100	\[
   101	|\mathcal S_n|\asymp\frac{P^2}{(\log P)^2}.
   102	\]
   103	Therefore
   104	\[
   105	\frac{E_n}{|\mathcal S_n|}
   106	\ll \frac{(\log P)^2}{H^2}
   107	\left(1+\frac{Y^2}{P}\right)
   108	\ll_\varepsilon X^{-\varepsilon},
   109	\]
   110	because \(H\asymp X^{\varepsilon/2}n\).
   111	
   112	**This repairs the exceptional-set proof without any short-interval prime theorem.**
