   182	**The order issue is substantive.**
   183	
   184	For every prime divisor \(p\),
   185	\[
   186	\operatorname{ord}_p(\beta^L)
   187	=\frac{\operatorname{ord}_p(\beta)}
   188	{\gcd(\operatorname{ord}_p(\beta),L)}.
   189	\]
   190	A large order of \(\beta\) does not imply adequate order after powering.
   191	
   192	An explicit balanced example is
   193	\[
   194	N=31\cdot43=1333,\qquad
   195	L=(3\cdot5\cdot7)^2=11025,\qquad \beta=3.
   196	\]
   197	Exact computation gives
   198	\[
   199	\beta^L\equiv-1\pmod{1333}.
   200	\]
   201	Moreover, the entire \(L\)-power image modulo each prime has size two:
   202	\[
   203	\frac{30}{\gcd(30,L)}
   204	=\frac{42}{\gcd(42,L)}=2.
   205	\]
   206	There is no \(L\)-th power of order greater than two on either component. For \(\gamma=-1\), the baby-order gcd checks yield either \(1\) or \(N\), and repetition gives `small_order`.
   207	
   208	This example concerns the common-power construction generally; it is not asserted to be a counterexample to the asymptotic theorem’s exact band design.
   209	
   210	A sufficient operational condition is baby-list injectivity modulo the relevant factor. With the existing gcd checks, certified \(\operatorname{ord}_N(\gamma)\ge m\) suffices to obtain either a factor or injectivity on both components. There is no need to demand order exceeding the absolute target exponents.

