   573	## 10. Prior art (GPT-6.1-sol r12 + dclt_1 + own WebSearch; curated)
   574	
   575	- **Ingredients (all prior art).**
   576	  - Weighted Fermat / Lehman searches: Lawrence 1895, Lehman 1974.
   577	  - Modulus-squared lock: McKee 1999 (heuristic N^{1/4}); HH 2022 Prop. 3.3.
   578	  - Shared one-sided collision search with multipoint evaluation: Harvey 2021,
   579	    Alg. 4.1–4.2.
   580	  - Arithmetic large sieve: Montgomery 1968; Montgomery–Vaughan 1973.
   581	  - Elementary counting of primes with small ord_q(2): divisors of 2^k − 1.
   582	- **Exponent below 1/5 for almost all bounded-ratio prime pairs (unconditional, deterministic):
   583	  no prior result found** by GPT-6.1-sol (two independent sweeps) or by our searches.
   584	  - The known bounds are 1/5 (Harvey 2021; HH 2022; Gao et al. 2512.19076 for balanced N,
   585	    log factors only).
   586	  - The conditional route is 1/6 under Umans–Wang's conjecture; He–Sahai 2026 refute its
   587	    rank-1 form.
   588	  - Almost-all unrestricted semiprimes are easy by trial division (Crişan–Erban counting), but
   589	    that does not apply to S_n.
   590	- **The clean-band trick** (disjoint prime bands so that one is coprime to q − 1, plus large
   591	  ord_q(2) for almost all q, plus a direct count of two-sided true giants) was not found in
   592	  the factoring papers checked.
   593	- **McKee–Pinch 1996 (ANTS II, LNCS 1122) §5, pp. 221–223: a direct precursor.** The r13
   594	  referee obtained it, and we read the scans ourselves: references/mckee_pinch_1996/
   595	  mckee-03.jpg and mckee-04.jpg; source https://cr.yp.to/bib/1996/mckee-deterministic.html.
   596	  - **Lehmer's speed-up.** If p ≡ q ≡ 1 (mod k), Fermat runs in O(q/k²).
   597	  - **The authors' extension.** For p ≡ ±q (mod k), Fermat runs in O(q/k^{2−ε}), via
   598	    n = u² − v² with u or v determined mod k² from square roots mod k. That is the
   599	    modulus-squared lock.
   600	  - **Multipliers.** If λp ≡ ±μq (mod k), the same method splits λμn in
   601	    O(max(λp, μq)/k²·n^ε).
   602	  - **A deterministic O(n^{1/3+ε}) algorithm.** Take k = 2⌊n^{1/3}⌋ + 1, and a pigeonhole
   603	    lemma that guarantees λμ ≤ k with λp ≡ ±μq (mod k).
   604	  - **A heuristic balanced method, "not however an algorithm".** Take primes k ∈ (n^{1/4},
   605	    2n^{1/4}) and small λ, μ, and assume λp − μq ≡ 0 (mod k) for some of them. Each (λμ, k)
   606	    costs O(n^ε). The expected running time O(n^{1/4+ε}) rests on the heuristic that q − p
   607	    has a prime factor in the band with probability ≫ 1/log n.
   608	  - **So the core event** (a band prime divides a small-weight combination Bp − Aq, with
   609	    N-determined residues and a modulus-squared reduction) **is theirs.**
   610	  - **What D′ adds:**
   611	    - (i) the band at N^{1/6}H^{−1/3} instead of N^{1/4}. This is made possible by
   612	    - (ii) one baby list shared by all band primes via the cofactor-powered handles
   613	      u_ℓ^{B+AN−c}, so each target costs one giant step against a window of size ≈ N^{1/6};
   614	    - (iii) a proof that the event happens for all but O(N^{−ε}) of the pairs: the
   615	      prime-pair large sieve with growing weight height H, replacing their probability
   616	      heuristic;
   617	    - (iv) one-sided extraction with no order hypothesis: the clean band, ord_q(2), and the
   618	      Lemma 10 count;
   619	    - (v) a full bit-complexity accounting.
   620	  - Their per-k cost is O(n^ε) because q/k² = O(1). Ours trades a larger window for fewer,
   621	    smaller primes through the shared baby list.
   622	- **Carella, arXiv 1308.2891.** It explicitly claims a deterministic exponent-1/6 factoring
   623	  algorithm.
   624	  - *The r13 referee's assessment, not independently re-derived by us:* the displayed proof
   625	    loses a necessary primitive-polynomial normalisation. Every coefficient of the correctly
   626	    expanded polynomial is divisible by B. Removing that content lowers the scaled height
   627	    from ≈ N to N^{5/6}, and the cited small-root condition then no longer supports
   628	    X = Y = N^{1/3}.
   629	  - The referee does not regard that proof as establishing the bound. We list it as a claimed
   630	    but (per that assessment) unproved prior statement of the same exponent.
   631	- **Claim limited to:** "no prior formulation found by us" of the *combination*: (i)–(v) above,
   632	  on top of McKee–Pinch §5's divisibility event and HH 2022 Prop. 3.3's lock. Novelty
   633	  judgments are left to the orchestrator.
