   635	## 11. Candidate card
   636	
   637	- **Mechanism.** DCL with one-sided extraction certified on a clean band (§2).
   638	  **Representation.** (Z/N)^*, base 2.
   639	- **Factor feature.** g = (Bp + Aq − c)/ℓ² in a whole-range window; the true giant equals
   640	  γ^{i*} mod p exactly.
   641	- **Outcome.**
   642	  - Proved: Theorem D′ and Corollary D″.
   643	  - Experimentally supported: correctness, every lemma, measured E slope 0.203 vs HH 0.226 at
   644	    48–80 bits, with HH 2.3–4.9× cheaper in absolute terms.
   645	  - Refuted for this model: none.
   646	  - Conjectured: e^{−λ} exceptional rate (Poisson), DCH (worst case).
   647	- **Scoped obstruction.**
   648	  - The large-sieve exceptional rate costs N^{5ε/6} for N^{−ε}.
   649	  - Worst case needs DCH plus large order mod the specific prime q on a clean band.
   650	  - Below 1/6 is blocked in the sumset model (multimod Thm A).
   651	- **Most promising open question.** Improve the exceptional-rate/cost trade-off beyond the
   652	  large sieve's n²/H².
   653	  - **Heuristic (not a theorem; qualified after r13).** For a fixed q, the p-interval has
   654	    length P ≈ Y³, so a sieve whose level is limited to ≈ P only sees moduli built from
   655	    ≤ 3 band primes. We *expect* that standard one-variable upper-bound sieves restricted to
   656	    such levels (Selberg, the large sieve) give only polynomial-in-1/λ savings, and this
   657	    explains the loss in our proof. We have **not** proved any barrier: other sieve weights,
   658	    bilinear or dispersion methods, or the averaging over q below might do better.
   659	  - Averaging over q gives a two-variable sieve over the box (classes Bp ≡ Aq mod ℓ, density
   660	    |V|/ℓ) with level up to P². That allows products of up to ~6 band primes and plausibly a
   661	    polynomial gain O((n/H²)^{c}) with c > 1 (**conjectured, not yet tested**).
   662	  - A Poisson-type e^{−cλ} bound would give N^{1/6}·polylog cost with N^{−ε} exceptions
