   170	- **Base order.** "Same windows" needs ord_p(b^{M^e}) to exceed the baby-list length.
   171	  In (Z/N)^* that order can be as small as (p−1)/gcd(M^e, p−1), whereas F_{p^f} has
   172	  more room. The lower bound below does not use the matched handle; it only uses the
   173	  inclusion of the target sets (cyclotomic targets ⊆ Λ ∪ L_{r,M}). So the cost
   174	  comparison is stated for parameter ranges where the (Z/N)^* order is not binding.
   175	  This holds for M^e ≪ p/W, which covers all calibrated algorithms.
   176	- **Multi-base searches.** Handles that combine several σ_s with s ∉ ⟨r⟩ as
   177	  *independent* search directions are multi-base vector-exponent searches. They lie
   178	  outside the 1-D cost model, as do multi-base searches in (Z/N)^*. See §3.5 for
   179	  their separable part.
   180	
   181	**The (Z/N)^* handle collapse is a feature, not a loss.** Z[p,q]/(pq−N, p−1) ≅ Z:
   182	one handle a^{c} serves every F with F(1,N) = c simultaneously.
   183	
   184	**Genuinely independent cyclotomic handles.** In the subgroup of order dividing
   185	Φ_d(p) (d | f), the exponent ring is Z[p,q]/(pq−N, Φ_d(p)) ≅ Z[x]/Φ_d(x), because
   186	q = N x^{−1} and x^{−1} is a polynomial mod Φ_d. It has rank φ(d). For example, for
   187	d = 4: p² ≡ −1, p³ ≡ −p, q ≡ −Np, q² ≡ −N². So only σ^0, …, σ^{φ(d)−1}, weighted by
   188	powers of N, are independent handles. This sharper structure means fewer targets
   189	share a handle, not more.
   190	
   191	Cost of making elements of order dividing Φ_d(p): β^{(p^d−1)/Φ_d(p)} =
   192	β^{∏_{e|d,e<d}Φ_e(p)}, a σ-product of degree < d (for d=4, codex's σ²(β)/β). It is
   193	useful only when the residue degree f is a multiple of d, and that is not guaranteed by
   194	the guess. All of this is pure overhead in the model, because of D1.
